WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.364 --> 00:00:17.489
"It's a perfect day to walk across the Brooklyn Bridge," Paul said.

2
00:00:18.629 --> 00:00:24.711
He looked up at the enormous bridge that connected two very busy parts of New York City—Brooklyn and

3
00:00:24.771 --> 00:00:27.732
Manhattan. The bridge was pretty old now.

4
00:00:28.204 --> 00:00:34.966
But with its tall stone towers and strong-looking cables, it was still an amazing sight above the East

5
00:00:35.026 --> 00:00:38.628
River. "Dad, who built this bridge?"

6
00:00:39.108 --> 00:00:39.768
Paul asked.

7
00:00:39.788 --> 00:00:44.189
"A man named John Roebling," his father answered.

8
00:00:44.209 --> 00:00:48.971
"Now Brooklyn and Manhattan are part of the same city—New York.

9
00:00:49.931 --> 00:00:51.832
But they used to be two different cities.

10
00:00:52.904 --> 00:00:58.386
In the 1800s boats would bring people back and forth across the East River to work, shop, or

11
00:00:58.426 --> 00:00:59.107
visit friends.

12
00:01:00.127 --> 00:01:03.108
The river was sometimes rough, and the boats were often late.

13
00:01:04.169 --> 00:01:08.430
One day John was on one of those boats with his son, and he got the idea to

14
00:01:08.470 --> 00:01:10.511
build a suspension bridge across the river.

15
00:01:11.492 --> 00:01:13.212
Many people said it couldn't be done.

16
00:01:14.533 --> 00:01:18.114
But one winter the East River froze, and the boats couldn't get through.

17
00:01:19.095 --> 00:01:21.596
People from Brooklyn couldn't get to work in Manhattan.

18
00:01:22.580 --> 00:01:26.882
Finally it was decided that a bridge should be built, connecting the two places."

19
00:01:26.902 --> 00:01:30.944
"Was John Roebling an engineer like you?"

20
00:01:31.644 --> 00:01:32.284
asked Paul.

21
00:01:32.304 --> 00:01:33.965
"Yes, he was.

22
00:01:34.565 --> 00:01:36.926
His son, Washington, was an engineer too.

23
00:01:37.727 --> 00:01:40.688
In 1869 they started to work on the bridge together.

24
00:01:41.408 --> 00:01:43.589
But sadly, a freak accident happened.

25
00:01:44.910 --> 00:01:49.432
While John Roebling was on the Brooklyn side of the river, his foot got caught in the dock.

26
00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:53.762
An incoming boat crashed into the dock, injuring him badly.

27
00:01:54.403 --> 00:01:55.544
He died a month later.

28
00:01:55.904 --> 00:01:57.125
So Washington took over."

29
00:01:57.145 --> 00:02:03.029
"Washington must have been sad when his father died," murmured Paul.

30
00:02:03.049 --> 00:02:06.531
"Yes, he and his father were very close.

31
00:02:07.091 --> 00:02:08.912
But Washington was ready for the job.

32
00:02:10.013 --> 00:02:15.197
He started working right away, digging deep under the river mud to create supports for the two bridge

33
00:02:15.257 --> 00:02:17.658
towers. This almost cost him his life."

34
00:02:18.739 --> 00:02:19.620
Paul gasped.

35
00:02:20.532 --> 00:02:23.475
"You mean, Washington almost died too?"

36
00:02:23.495 --> 00:02:29.099
"Yes, he designed something called a caisson to use for the underwater project.

37
00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:35.345
These caissons, which prevented the water from flooding the work area, were filled with compressed air.

38
00:02:36.506 --> 00:02:42.791
And at the time, people didn't realize that working in compressed air can cause caisson disease, or decompression

39
00:02:42.831 --> 00:02:48.356
sickness. That's a dangerous condition that results from gas bubbles forming inside the body.

40
00:02:49.396 --> 00:02:55.079
Caisson disease affected Washington's nervous system, paralyzing him for a time and causing him great pain.

41
00:02:56.180 --> 00:02:58.961
Some members of his work crew even died from the illness!

42
00:03:00.042 --> 00:03:03.944
Washington never returned to the bridge, but he continued to manage the project.

43
00:03:05.565 --> 00:03:10.808
Imagine this—he used a telescope to watch the progress of the bridge construction from his home in Brooklyn!"

44
00:03:10.828 --> 00:03:17.871
"Wow. It sounds like he was pretty sick—how could he manage everything from home?"

45
00:03:18.311 --> 00:03:18.972
Paul asked.

46
00:03:20.012 --> 00:03:21.993
"Good question," his father said.

47
00:03:22.013 --> 00:03:24.914
"Washington's wife, Emily, helped him.

48
00:03:25.934 --> 00:03:28.115
She wrote his letters and helped with planning the work.

49
00:03:29.296 --> 00:03:33.137
She served as his eyes and ears, and delivered all his orders to the workers.

50
00:03:34.298 --> 00:03:37.679
She even studied complicated mathematics to learn more about building!

51
00:03:38.860 --> 00:03:41.901
Soon she earned the respect of everyone involved in the project.

52
00:03:42.941 --> 00:03:46.403
If it weren't for her, the Brooklyn Bridge probably wouldn't have been finished."

53
00:03:47.403 --> 00:03:49.644
Paul's father pointed to the bridge towers.

54
00:03:50.636 --> 00:03:56.158
"When those towers were finished, they were 25 stories high, taller than any of the other buildings around

55
00:03:56.198 --> 00:03:57.639
them. Can you imagine that?"

56
00:03:58.959 --> 00:04:00.740
Paul looked at the city skyline.

57
00:04:01.841 --> 00:04:05.562
All the huge skyscrapers made the bridge towers look small now.

58
00:04:06.642 --> 00:04:09.904
He tried to imagine how different the city must have looked back then.

59
00:04:09.924 --> 00:04:15.166
"See those four main cables that run along the top from one tower to the other?"

60
00:04:15.546 --> 00:04:16.566
his father went on.

61
00:04:16.586 --> 00:04:20.188
"Can you spot all the wires hanging down from them?

62
00:04:20.828 --> 00:04:24.331
John Roebling invented their material, which is called wire rope.

63
00:04:25.511 --> 00:04:29.935
The wire rope is made of steel, so it is strong and can easily be bent into any

64
00:04:30.015 --> 00:04:33.937
shape. The Brooklyn Bridge was the first bridge where it was used.

65
00:04:34.938 --> 00:04:37.380
But those slanted supports were Washington's idea.

66
00:04:38.561 --> 00:04:40.522
He added them to make sure the bridge was safe."

67
00:04:41.603 --> 00:04:43.944
"How long did it take to build the bridge?"

68
00:04:44.365 --> 00:04:45.025
asked Paul.

69
00:04:45.045 --> 00:04:48.688
"Fourteen years," answered his father.

70
00:04:49.992 --> 00:04:51.713
"It was an amazing undertaking.

71
00:04:52.313 --> 00:04:55.835
When it was finished, it was the longest suspension bridge in the world.

72
00:04:57.136 --> 00:05:03.259
At the opening day ceremony in 1883, Emily Roebling was given the honor of being the first person

73
00:05:03.279 --> 00:05:04.180
to cross the bridge.

74
00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:08.842
She rode in a horse and carriage from the Brooklyn side to the Manhattan side.

75
00:05:09.843 --> 00:05:11.904
The workers all cheered as she went by."

76
00:05:11.924 --> 00:05:16.727
"Her husband must have been proud," said Paul.

77
00:05:16.747 --> 00:05:19.188
"Yes, it was his idea.

78
00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:25.282
Crowds lined the streets of Manhattan and Brooklyn, and hundreds of ships were packed into the East River.

79
00:05:26.302 --> 00:05:31.384
On the Manhattan side, the president of the United States waited with the governor and the mayor of

80
00:05:31.464 --> 00:05:32.164
New York City.

81
00:05:33.204 --> 00:05:36.545
Then they met Emily and they all walked across the bridge together.

82
00:05:37.786 --> 00:05:41.787
After the ceremony they went to the Roebling house to honor Washington and his father.

83
00:05:42.807 --> 00:05:45.148
There was a grand show of fireworks that night.

84
00:05:46.184 --> 00:05:49.846
It was the greatest celebration ever held in America up until that time."

85
00:05:49.866 --> 00:05:53.927
"That sounds pretty cool," said Paul.

86
00:05:54.587 --> 00:05:56.148
His father agreed with a nod.

87
00:05:56.168 --> 00:06:00.990
"I'd like to help build a bridge like that one day," said Paul.

88
00:06:02.190 --> 00:06:03.351
His father beamed.

89
00:06:03.371 --> 00:06:10.374
"The Brooklyn Bridge inspired the building of many other bridges, allowing people to travel easily to many new

90
00:06:10.414 --> 00:06:15.896
places. It was seen as the greatest engineering achievement of the nineteenth century."

91
00:06:16.956 --> 00:06:21.382
"My teacher said the Brooklyn Bridge is a true American landmark."

92
00:06:22.583 --> 00:06:24.205
Paul grabbed his father's arm.

93
00:06:24.225 --> 00:06:25.627
"Come on, Dad!

94
00:06:26.087 --> 00:06:29.031
Let's see how fast we can get to the other side!"
